- `sharesHousehold` (lines 333-337): no constraint (write-only Boolean flag)

Important: the DTO's `telephone` constraint is only `@NotNull @Size(1,20)` — the E.164 `@Pattern` shown on the *entity* (`Owner.telephone`) does NOT run on the request body, because the entity is never bean-validated on this path (see §6 note). The E.164 enforcement happens via the controller's manual `normalizeTelephone`.

### 2b. The persistence entity `Owner` and its constraints (present but NOT triggered on POST)

`/home/daniel/sandbox/src/main/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/model/Owner.java` — `@Entity @Table(name="owners")` extends `Person`.
Field-level Bean Validation constraints declared on the entity (lines 43-77):
- `address`: `@NotEmpty`
- `city`: `@NotEmpty`
- `telephone`: `@NotEmpty @Pattern(regexp = "^\\+[0-9]{8,15}$", message="Phone number must be in E.164 form …")`
- `email`: `@Email`
- `postcode`: `@Pattern(regexp = "^[0-9]{4}$", message="Postcode must be 4 digits")`

Superclass `/home/daniel/sandbox/src/main/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/model/Person.java` (lines 31-37): `firstName` `@NotEmpty`, `lastName` `@NotEmpty`.

These entity constraints are effectively dormant on the create path — the controller never wraps the entity in `@Valid`, and JPA Bean Validation on flush is not configured (no `@PrePersist`/validation lifecycle). The setters do meaningful normalization though: `setAddress` normalizes/upper-cases/expands abbreviations (lines 182-236), `setEmail` lower-cases (lines 288-290). `getAddress()` (lines 164-172) composes structured `addressLine1`/`addressLine2` over the flat `address`, which the controller relies on. Many derived values (`getIdentityKey`, `getMemberId` inputs, `getMembershipLevel`, `isPostcodeValidForCity`, `getLocality`) are computed on the entity and used by the controller.

